A||N||33||Taijiguy||What has happened to music??||||11||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||05-28-2001||10:29 PM||||So I'm sitting here bored, watching whatever music award show this is on TV. Right now there's some girl who sounds like a smurf singing. I swear, music has no soul any more. I play guitar, I'm not great, but I love it. Mostly blues. The people who inspire me to play put their heart and soul into the music. Today I have no idea what it is, but it lacks passion. <BR>I love Eric Clapton's comment about, even a sour note is good, if it's played with emotion. <P>(I just figure when I play a sour note it's <I>jazz</I>)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000001||Philphine||05-28-2001||10:46 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.wfpk.org" TARGET=_blank>www.wfpk.org</A> <P>our local public radio station. they do streaming audio now. i can't get it on web tv but if it's like regular radio i think you'll like it. they say they can't do some of their stuff that come from syndicated sourses but the variety is great.<P>the only thing is, when i tell someone about it and say they like country, thats the time they play some hard core reggae. or if they like rock, then when they switch to it, it's ear bending bluegrass. but if you listen for a while the variety grows on you. makes it hard to listen to a regular station for very long.||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||05-28-2001||11:37 PM||||What happened to music?<P>Boy bands and teenage pop queens.<P>It'll pass. Lame fads always do.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">    <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||05-28-2001||11:43 PM||||I never ever listen to that pop music crap.  Nor rap either.  Though there is some rap that I can somewhat appreciate, most of the kids my age are, IMO, listening to absolute crap.<P>I listen to classic rock.  I love it.  Those artists actually had some talent.  Especially Jimi Hendrix, talk about a real rocker.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cheever2||05-28-2001||11:56 PM||||I try to refrain from asking that question, because it's exactly what my dad said when I was the age my kids are now.  I liked the Classic Rock mentioned above, except that it wasn't "classic" then.<P>Now that I'm older I've found my music, and it's jazz, as discussed in this old thread... <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/000847.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/000847.html</A> <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000005||Taijiguy||05-29-2001||12:17 AM||||I looked over the post, and noticed you mentioned Jean Luc Ponty. I first became aware of him in the Zappa song 50/50 from (I think) Overnight sensation. He had an incredible solo in that song, as bizarre as it was. I was hooked and bought everything I could find of his. The Zappa album "Weasles Ripped My Flesh" was an unbelievable jazz album. (Albeit an odd title for such)<BR>If you like good jazz, a few other guys to check out: Stanley Clark, and Stanley Jordan. Also, there's an album out called Three Strings, it's Jean Luc Ponty, Stanley Clark, and Al DiMeola. There's also another outstanding acoustic album; John McLaughlin, Al DiMeola, Paco DeLucia. Incredible work. Three acoustic guitars, and that's it. No drums, bass, or other instruments. Just three amazing guitar players filling the air. <BR>For the serious guitar rock fans I suggest "G-3 Live"; Joe Satriani, Eric Johnson, and Steve Vai. They do three songs each individually, then collaborate at the end to cover three of the best rock songs ever: Frank Zappa's "My Guitar Wants to Kill Your Mama", Jeff Beck's "Goin' Down", and Jimi Hendrix "Red House". Amazing.||||reg||1
Z||000006||btoth||05-29-2001||12:27 AM||||Music has just become a series of fads more than ever.  I don't even listen to the radio anymore because all you hear is the same crap over and over again.  I listen mainly to hard-rock (generalizing)... but I try to diversify once in a while.<P>What I listen to the most is Tool, Nine Inch Nails, REM and Tori Amos - "To Venus and Back".  <P>Tool is my all time favorite... it's hard to explain why. Read a review.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I never really enjoyed NIN until one day I forced myself to listen to "The Fragile" all the way through... since then, I've been hooked.  <BR>REM has been around forever... and they never seem to want to stick to any particular style, which I give them credit for.  And as far as that Tori Amos cd... not sure, but it's just sounds good to me some days.<P>Anymore though, I have a real hard time paying for a CD that I know will just be played to death on the radio.  Maybe that's why the music I enjoy the most is that that isn't played at all (at least in Cleveland).<P><B>Taijiguy</B>, Joe Satriani's "Crystal Planet" is pretty cool.  I particularly enjoy "Psycho Monkey".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 05-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||05-29-2001||12:38 AM||||Let me quote a line from a song from my genre of music... Country... I like what the song says, although it does tend to fall a little into the pop country field...<P>'There oughta be a law against cowboy rap, and all that boy band crap. A little sissy in a cowboy hat ain't country...'<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||05-29-2001||12:45 AM||||<B>btoth:</B><BR>Crystal Planet is OK, but I think my favorite Satch album is "Surfing With The Alien". And there's a song on another album (I don't recal which, I have about 10 of his) It's called "I Want What I Want" that I love. I saw him first when he had all his hair. Then I saw him again a few years later, and he was bald as an egg. I almost peed myself he looked so funny.<BR>A footnote:<BR>A couple of years ago I saw Jeff Beck open for Jethro Tull. I have to tell you, he owned that place. Tull was boring after listening to Jeff strangle his guitar for an hour. What an awsome show.||||reg||14
Z||000009||FieroJoe||05-29-2001||07:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>'There oughta be a law against cowboy rap, and all that boy band crap. A little sissy in a cowboy hat ain't country...'<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Aint that the truth.  Since at work we must listen to the country station, for the last year and a half I have grown accustomed to it.  There are some songs that I actually like on there too.  A few of them can get down right annoying, mostly becuase the radio station plays the same song 2 or 3 times within an hour.  But still, I find myself switching to the country station every once and a while; the music's not bad at all. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||14
Z||000010||rogergarrison||05-29-2001||08:05 AM||||If I turn on my radio at all, its mostly for talk radio like Art Bell. Ya a little music is OK, country or sorry to say Brit. Sorry, but I do kinda like songs with words IF I have to listen. And MTV would never be on my tv if it wasnt for her, Christina, Mandy, Mariah.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Taijiguy||05-29-2001||08:12 AM||||Roger, suddenly your motives are obvious. If you were blind you wouldn't listen to any of them. LOL <p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-29-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000012||Cheever2||05-29-2001||08:29 AM||||<B>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR>Jean Luc Ponty. I was hooked and bought everything I could find of his.</B>Then you probably have his King Kong album.  If not, and you want it, I can send it to you, cuz I'm tired of it now.<P><B>a few other guys to check out: Stanley Clark, and Stanley Jordan.</B>I know of them but somehow never tried any of their stuff.<P><B> Also, there's an album out called Three Strings, it's Jean Luc Ponty, Stanley Clark, and Al DiMeola.</B> I never heard of this, but I must check it out. <P><B> acoustic album: John McLaughlin, Al DiMeola, Paco DeLucia.</B>I had heard of this one but never got it.  Maybe I will now.<P>Thanks, Taij, for those reviews.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||p4n1c||05-29-2001||08:48 AM||||I agree, most new music coming out today is crap.  Most rap is horrible and pop makes me want to punk.  The new punk bands coming out shouldn't be called punk, it's to damn mainstream and conforming.<P>I listen to mainly the punk bands that have been around a long time, The Offspring(1986) and NOFX(~1985).  The Vandals, Guttermouth, No Use for a Name, Gob(Canadian), Larger than Life(mp3.com band), Whippersnapper(mp3.com band), 88 Fingers Louie, Pennywise, and any other good hard punk band.<P>Only listen to some Blink 182 and Green Day(read: Diaper Punk).<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"||||reg||1
Z||000014||Patrick's Dad||05-29-2001||09:31 AM||||Boy, someone's touched a nerve here....<P>Popular music, now as always, is an expression of who pays the bills. Mozart wrote for the church, then the royal court because he was on retainer. Much of his best music (Eine kleine nachtmusik, and, even The Magic Flute) were his own expressions that music was for everyone. At least, that's how I've translated it....<P>As to jazz, I've just begun to scratch the surface, but I'm leaning to some of the older stuff; Coltrane, Monk, and Charlie Parker. When my new (to me) tube preamp comes in, this is what I'll listen to first. Chuck Mangione's "Live at the Hollywood Bowl," too.<P>For today's stuff, it's Creed (In a big way). "My Own Prison" is amazing, and "One" is way too catchy. Plus the music means something. I also listen to Mariah, but mostly her older stuff. In the words of a (younger) coworker, she's become a "hoochie." She was much cuter back then, and her songs were at once art and craft. Most were good at it, some weren't so good, but they were hers. Now, she takes up with alomst anyone (musically) and lets them play. Prince's "The Beautiful Ones" was a disaster on her Butterfly album. I'm a huge Prince fan from way back, although I don't play any of it when the kids are home. However, "All in Your Mind" from Mariah's first album overshadows almost everything else she's done.<P>I grew up in the days of The Commodores, Earth, Wind, & Fire, George Clinton, Parliament, Funkadelic, and Kool & the Gang before they went "pop." I was funky when my friends were listening to Aerosmith, Zepplin, The Doors, and Van Halen. Later in life, I gained an appreciation for V*F*H. "Diver Down" is a great album.<P>Rant, rant, rant....    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000015||Taijiguy||05-29-2001||09:35 AM||||I enjoy alot of new music. I love Kid Rock, and 'Bizkit. Those guys have a great sense of humor. Metallica is about as intelligent as rock gets, they have some incredible lyrics. I also enjoy some alternative/metal; Korn, Tool, Corrosion of Conformity, and others are great for working out, driving, and the garage, very aggresive. And there's alot of talent there. Not a big fan of rap or country, and for sure pop sucks. I have to admit, Ice-T has a hell of a good rap influenced rock band called Body Count, who covered "Hey Joe" on a Hendrix tribute album. They're definietly palitable, sort of similar to Living Color. (which I also consider to be a great band)<P>Patrick's Dad: You and I were responding at the same time. If you enjoyed "Diver Down" check out "Mean Streets", and "Women and Children First". Two VH albums from the early 80's that got little airplay, but have some of their best music as far as I'm concerned. Definitely worth a listen  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||JSocha||05-29-2001||09:50 AM||||&lt;Eddie&gt; Van Halen rules!<P>Just my opinion.<P>Of course I also like AC/DC, KISS, Joe Satriani, Motley Crue, Poison, Keel, Bon Jovi, Slaughter, Quiet Riot, Headpins (another band that ruled), Dokken, Ted Nugent, White Snake, Aerosmith on and on and on and on.<P>If anybody is interested, check out this link of events happening this June here in the hills (Turtle Mountains).<BR> <A HREF="http://www.rockinthehills.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rockinthehills.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Philphine||05-29-2001||11:09 AM||||another public radio plug. i hear a lot of people who you might think have stopped making music on public radio. like van morrison or joni mitchell, who almost put out music every year. and also people and groups that don't quite fit into any regular radio station format. plus every once in a while i get to be on the cutting edge when regular radio starts paying something i heard long before.<P>like shelby lynne. she won best new artist at the last grammys but she's had a few albums out. she wasn't fitting in to the nashville scene well so she put out an album that dosen't really fit well on any station. it's a great cd. or johnatha brooke, who's kind of getting big now but has had 6 albums including a couple with a band she was in.<P>did i mention i like public radio?||||reg||1
Z||000018||btoth||05-29-2001||11:23 AM||||I've found my college radio station to be a great way to hear different stuff... but it doesn't come in too good on the car radio.<P>I never really listen to jazz... my dad loves it though (that and Pink Floyd).  There is this one cd he has, I think they're called "Down to the Bone" (?),  that's very catchy.  And it has some nice bass to it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||FieroLisa||05-29-2001||01:43 PM||||You guys have to remember when you were younger, everyone thought your music was crap! Some day, Backstreet boys will be on the Oldie's stations and it will be considered "when music was good" or something along those lines. I listen to a lot of music: country, rap, r&b, whatever is on that I like. There's a lot that I don't like, but I just change the station, not complain about it. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Slammed Fiero||05-29-2001||02:18 PM||||backstreet boys will never be condsidered good music. They will go the way of New Kids on the Block. they sucked when they were popular and they still suck<P>So whats everyones favortie bands / songs<BR>me:<P>Weezer<BR>Cake <BR>Toadies<BR>CCR<BR>I listen to everything , I drove a delivery truck for a summer and all the radio got was oldies , so I even listen to that on occasion , the reception area at the hospital where my girlfriend works always plays oldies , My brother listens to Classical so I listen to that on occasion..<P>anything with a good beat..even techno/ house music.<P> <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.<p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 05-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||p4n1c||05-29-2001||02:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B>who covered "Hey Joe" on a Hendrix tribute album. They're definietly palitable, sort of similar to Living Color. (which I also consider to be a great band)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That song has been covered a number of times in the past.  The Offspring covered it on their Baghdad EP and I've heard a few other renditions.<P>I can't stand rappers that do covers of old songs because they end up ruining it(read: Puff Daddy).  Actually a couple of older songs done by punk bands should not have been done(ie. Walk like an Egyptian by 88 Fingers/All against Authority, it was horrible.  Want you to Want me by NOFX(it was damn funny, but horrible)).<P>Covers should be close to the original.  Sure the singer is going to sound different, but it should still be similar(ie. I Wanna be Sedated, Smash it Up, Blood Stains, Total Immortal, Ballroom Blitz by The Offspring.  They were all very well done and close to original with just a bit of hard/fast punk thrown in).<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"||||reg||1
Z||000022||Taijiguy||05-29-2001||02:30 PM||||Lisa:<BR>To an extent, you're probably right. And maybe in the discussion of our favorite music, my original thoughts were lost. <BR>To me, alot of music today just lacks the committment and dedication of old. This smurfette who was singing last night, she couldn't sing to save her life. Most of these pop singers, if it weren't for electronic processing of their voices, no one would be able to stand them. And the musicians, so many are "three chord wonders" who if pressed to play an improvisation would probably stare at you like a dog watching a cieling fan. <BR>Now, I'm not saying it "bad' per se. Obviously, those people have a following. I guess I was as much as anything just expressing that I prefer music with substance. Where practice and effort make a difference. Where the talent is real, not purchased technology.||||reg||1
Z||000023||p4n1c||05-29-2001||02:36 PM||||Watch where you use the term "three chord wonder", Sex Pistols, The Ramones and The Clash were the original "three chord wonders".  The Offspring are "three chord wonders", and Noodles can improvise just fine.  He's played everything from alternative to heavy metal.  Electric to 12 string acoustic.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>I think the "bands" they have for the pop stars are just people that like the money.  They probably could play better, but they get paid well to be nameless and provide background.  They are definitly not "three chord wonders" though, they are useless twits with no real care about real music.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"||||reg||1
Z||000024||Taijiguy||05-29-2001||02:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> Originally posted by p4n1c:<BR><B>I can't stand rappers that do covers of old songs because they end up ruining it...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I agree with that 100%. The difference with this cover is, that this is a different band from his rap group. This is absolutely a rock band. They do some rap influenced stuff, but it's no doubt rock. (i.e. good guitar work, solid rock drumming, but with a R&B influence) This is a respectable rock cover, not a hip-hop cover. ||||reg||14
Z||000025||btoth||05-29-2001||02:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B><BR>To me, alot of music today just lacks the committment and dedication of old. This smurfette who was singing last night, she couldn't sing to save her life. Most of these pop singers, if it weren't for electronic processing of their voices, no one would be able to stand them. And the musicians, so many are "three chord wonders" who if pressed to play an improvisation would probably stare at you like a dog watching a cieling fan. <BR>Now, I'm not saying it "bad' per se. Obviously, those people have a following. I guess I was as much as anything just expressing that I prefer music with substance. Where practice and effort make a difference. Where the talent is real, not purchased technology.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So true.  I'm sick of "radio-friendly" music (as I call it) where each song follows the same format.  3 chords played over and over again behind stupid lyrics.  And of course, they have to have the "hook" that everyone can remember after the song is over... take Staind's "Outside" for instance:  if I hear "I'm on the outside, I'm looking in..." one more time, I'll scream.  It's a good song, but still....<P>Maybe that's why I'm so obsessed with Tool.  Cleveland radio station won't play their music, music video channels won't play their videos and most of their songs are long enough that people without enough patience will not bother listening to their music.  They're artistic and the songs have much more depth to them than those "radio-friendly" songs.  (Heck, I'm listening to their new cd right now... "Lateralus".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)  And they're kinda mysterious, too... they focus more on the music and art than who is behind the music.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Taijiguy||05-29-2001||03:26 PM||||Doesn't their lead guitarist play a 7 string guitar? ||||reg||1
